data.content:
# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `SetPropertyCalls<T>` → `SetPropertyBuilder<T>`

The type was renamed. All references in your expression-tree code must change:

```csharp
// Before (EF Core 9)
Expression<Func<SetPropertyCalls<Blog>, SetPropertyCalls<Blog>>> setters = ...

// After (EF Core 10)
Expression<Func<SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>, SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>>> setters = ...
```

Also update the `nameof(SetPropertyCalls<Blog>.SetProperty)` → `nameof(SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>.SetProperty)` and the `typeof(SetPropertyCalls<Blog>)` references if any exist in reflection/expression code.

## 2. `SetProperty` value parameter must now be a lambda

The overload taking a raw value (`TProperty value`) was removed. Both arguments must be `Expression<Func<TSource, TProperty>>` lambdas:

```csharp
// Before
s => s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8)
// After
s => s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, b => 8)
```

For your expression-tree building, replace `Expression.Constant("foo")` with a lambda expression:

```csharp
var sParam = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b2");
var valueLambda = Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
    Expression.Constant("foo"), sParam);

setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>, SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>>>(
    Expression.Call(
        instance: setters.Body,
        methodName: nameof(SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>.SetProperty),
        typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) },
        arguments: new Expression[]
        {
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
            valueLambda   // ← lambda, not Expression.Constant
        }),
    setters.Parameters);
```

## 3. Complex types — default column naming now includes the navigation name

EF Core 10 changed the default column name format from `Property` to `NavigationName_Property` consistently. If you have an existing database, you'll get migration mismatches. Fix by pinning column names:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, ab => {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingAddress_Street");
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ab => {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_Street");
    });
});

// For nested complex types, apply recursively:
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, pb => {
        pb.ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress, ab => {
            ab.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("Payment_BillingAddress_City");
        });
    });
});
```

Or opt into the old convention globally:

```csharp
options.UseColumnNameConvention(ColumnNameConvention.Legacy)
```

## 4. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `CommandSource` parameter

The interface method now requires a `CommandSource` parameter:

```csharp
// After (EF Core 10)
public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
    IRelationalConnection connection,
    DbCommand command,
    DbContext context,
    Guid commandId,
    Guid connectionId,
    DateTimeOffset startTime,
    CommandSource commandSource)   // ← new parameter
{ /* logging */ }
```

---

**Summary of required changes:**

| Area | What broke | Fix |
|------|-----------|-----|
| Type rename | `SetPropertyCalls<T>` | → `SetPropertyBuilder<T>` |
| SetProperty API | Raw value overload removed | Wrap values in lambdas (`b => 8`) |
| Complex types | Column naming convention changed | Pin names with `HasColumnName` or use legacy convention |
| Diagnostics | Interface method signature grew | Add `CommandSource` parameter |
